In this tutorial we'll discuss continous trait evolution and introduce the most basic statistical model of this process --- the Brownian motion model. By the end of this tutorial you'll be able to:

1. Define a continuous trait
2. Simulate a Brownian motion process and understand its basic properties
3. Simulate Brownian motion evolution through a branching lineage
4. Understand the difference between Brownian motion and Ornstein-Uhlenbeck processes

## Preamble: Continuous Traits, Phenomenological Models, and Stochastic Processes

Recall that a continuous trait is a trait that does not jump between values as it changes. For example, a palm tree will not jump from being 10 feet tall to 11 feet tall - as it grows, it passes through each value between these numbers. Height is thus a continuous trait. Traits such as limb length, root depth, or beak width are continuous. Traits such as number of molars and pollination type are discrete.

The values of continuous traits ultimately evolve due to microevolutionary processes - that is, changes in allele frequencies in populations due to migration, mutation, selection, and drift. Explicit mathematical models can be used to understand continuous trait evolution in populations over microevolutionary timescales based on the relative strength of these 4 processes and attributes of the population (such as population size, trait heritability between generations, etc.). Such models are routinely used in the subfields of population genetics and quantitative genetics, which are concerned with the study of evolution over relatively short periods.

At the macroevolutionary scale, however, two factors complicate the analysis of continuous trait evolution. First, we're usually interested in the change of a trait in entire species lineages, not populations. Second, we're interested in the evolution of a trait across timescales that are enormous relative to an organism's generation time. For example, we may be interested in changes in the AVERAGE beak length in a species of finch over 10 million years, where that species has been comprised of many populations that have variously arisen, fused, and gone extinct over many tens of thousands of generations. It is therefore impossible to analyze this process using explicit population genetic models. 

Our solution in macroevolution is to use statistcal models of continuous trait change that APPROXIMATE the behaviour we would expect to see under certain evolutionary processes over long periods of time. These models are called 'phenomenological' models, meaning they are not derived directly from first principles (i.e., laws of population genetics), but they are broadly consistent with those principles. 

The phenomenological models we use to study continuous trait evolution over macroevolutionary timescales are called 'stochastic process models'. These are mathematical models in which the change in a trait over large timescales involves some degree of randomness (note that some microevolutionary models also incorporate stochastic processes). The models can't tell us what the exact value of a species mean trait will be after some time period, but they can tell us what the statistical distribution of mean trait values will look like if we were to allow the trait to evolve many many times under a given process. The statistical approximation of trait evolution through time is the core practice of macroevolutionary analysis of phenotypes.

