## Exercise 2: The Brownian motion process in a branching lineage - Let's add a tree!

Of course, our interest in the Brownian motion model is for its application to trait evolution in a diversifying lineage. Now, we'll look explicitly at BM evolution in a phylogenetic tree.
	
We'll start by simulating a random walk in a branching lineage. We're not going to do this one by scratch - we'll use a function from Liam Revell's "phytools" package, which animates the branching process.

```{r eval=F}
branching.diffusion(sig2=0.1,b=0.023,time.stop=100,ylim=NULL,smooth=FALSE,pause=0.1,record=NULL,path=NULL)		
```

Here we can see that lineage diversification leads to the accumulation of trait variance over time under the BM model, much in the way that we observed an increase in variance with time when we repeated Brownian motion simulations independently from a common starting point. The structure of the phylogenetic tree, however, makes a big difference in how much variance accumulation we expect to observe.
	
Let's conduct one more set of simulations to explore how "tree shape" influences expected disparity. This time, we're going to start with known trees, and we'll simulate trait data using existing R packages.

```{r}
set.seed(3)
```

The line above sets R's random number generator to the start point '3'. A common start point is required so that we all work with the same values in this exercise.

Now we can simulate a branching tree --- we will say that evolution occurs across 60 my and that the speciation rate is 0.1. Note that the speciation rate is in units of number of branching events per million years per lineage --- the more lineages that are present, the more branching events that are possible. 

```{r}
base.tree <- sim.bd.age(age=60, numbsim=1, lambda=0.1, mu=0) #note that mu is the extinction rate
base.tree <- base.tree[[1]]		
```

Let's take a look at what type of object we've just created

```{r}
class(base.tree)		
```

This is a phylo object, which we've seen before.

Now we'll take this tree, and WITHOUT CHANGING THE TOPOLOGY, we'll rescale it so that the branching events occur late. We'll use the delta tree rescaling parameter to do this. When delta = 1, no rescaling occurs. When delta < 1, the tree is rescaled so that branching events occur late (towards the tips), and when delta > 1, the tree is rescaled so that branching events occur relatively early (towards the root). Note that 'rescale' is a function in both the phytools and plotrix packages, but those functions do very different things. We can tell R which package's 'rescale' we're reerring to by using the :: operator

```{r}
tree1 <- phytools::rescale(base.tree, model="delta", 0.15)		
```

Next, we'll again take our starting tree, and without changing the topology, we'll rescale it so that the branching events occur early.

```{r}
tree2 <- phytools::rescale(base.tree,model="delta",5)		
```

Let's plot them to see the differences in "tree shape".

```{r}
par(mfrow=c(2,1), mar=c(1,1,1,1))
plot(tree1,show.tip.label=F,edge.color="blue")
plot(tree2,show.tip.label=F,edge.color="red")	
```

So we have two trees with identical topologies, but where the timing of divergence varies. How does this affect expected disparity?	That's what we're going to see. In this case, by 'disparity' we mean the total amount of 'trait space' occupied by a clade (note that there are other ways to measure disparity, which simply means trait diversity). 

*So which tree shows taxa with more shared evolutionary history? Which shows more independent evolutionary history?*

*Given this knowledge, if trait evolution occurs via Brownian motion, which tree would you expect to give rise to greater trait disparity?*


We can simulate trait evolution across our two trees under a Brownian motion process using the function fastBM from the phytools package. 

We'll simulate two traits for each clade and plot them against each other to visualize disparity. 
Note that we are using a single instantaneous BM rate parameter (sig2=4) for every simulation, so the only thing that differs is the branching times of the trees (which, again, have identical topologies)

```{r}
par(mfrow=(c(1,1)))
		
tree1.trait1 <- fastBM(tree1, a=0, sig2=4, nsim=1) #a = the starting trait value (i.e., at the root node)
tree1.trait2 <- fastBM(tree1, a=0, sig2=4, nsim=1)
	
tree2.trait1 <- fastBM(tree2, a=0, sig2=4, nsim=1)
tree2.trait2 <- fastBM(tree2, a=0, sig2=4, nsim=1)

plot(x=tree1.trait1,y=tree1.trait2,xlim=c(-30,30),ylim=c(-30,30),xlab="Trait 1",ylab="Trait 2",pch=16,col="blue")
points(x=tree2.trait1,y=tree2.trait2,pch=16,col="red")

#so each point here represents a species value for two traits (lets say height and limb length)

# It's probably easier to visualize the difference in disparity this with 'minimum convex polygons', so let's plot some.

mcp2 <- cbind(tree2.trait1,tree2.trait2)
hull2 <- chull(mcp2)
hull2 <- c(hull2, hull2[1])
polygon(mcp2[hull2, ], density = NA, border = "lightcoral", col = "lightcoral")
				
mcp1 <- cbind(tree1.trait1,tree1.trait2)
hull1 <- chull(mcp1)
hull1 <- c(hull1, hull1[1])
polygon(mcp1[hull1, ], density = NA, border = "deepskyblue", col = "deepskyblue")

points(x=tree1.trait1,y=tree1.trait2,pch=16,col="blue")
points(x=tree2.trait1,y=tree2.trait2,pch=16,col="red")
```

*Which tree produces greater disparity?*


*If you wanted disparity to be similar for the blue clade and the red clade, what would you change about the Brownian motion model or the phylogeny?*




## Exercise 3: The Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process in a single lineage

While the Brownian Motion process is the grandfather of all macroevolutionary models of continuous traits, it is not the only model. A simple extension of the BM model is the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model (OU).

The OU process models a constrained random walk, unlike the BM process. In an OU process, the trait is effectively "pulled" toward a particular value, and the farther away it gets from this value, the stronger the pull becomes. Imagine an object that is tethered to a stationary point by a rubber band - this is a lot like how OU acts (e.g., the rubber band gets tighter, and exerts a stronger pull, with greater distance from the stationary point).

This dynamic mimics what we would expect from adaptation, so we can use this model to approximate the evolution of a trait that is under natural selection towards some value that we interpret to be the long-term adaptive optimum.

The OU model contains the same sig2 parameter as the BM model, but it also contains a second parameter, "alpha", which represents the strength of the constraint on the random walk, and a third parameter, "theta", which represents the optimum value around which evolution is constrained.
		
First we'll define the length of time over which to model evolution, the number of simulations, our model parameters, and the y-axis limits.

```{r}
time = 8
N = 100
sig2 = 0.09
alpha = 0.4
theta = 0
yl = c(-2, 2)
```

Now we can simulate evolution under both a BM and OU model and plot the outcomes.
Instead of doing this from scratch, we can use the random_walks function of the diverge package.
Note that in the code below we introduce custom x-axes and use custom axis titles.

```{r}
par(mfrow=c(2,1), mar=c(4,4,1,1))
# Brownian Motion
rw_bm = random_walks(model="BM_null", TIME=time, steps=10, nsim=N, sig2=sig2, plot=TRUE, col=rep("black",2), ylim=yl, xaxt="n", labels=F)
axis(1, at=seq(0, 80, 20), labels=seq(0, 8, 2))
title(xlab="Time (Ma)", ylab="Trait Value")

#Ornstein-Uhlenbeck
rw_ou = random_walks(model="OU_null", TIME=time, steps=10, nsim=N, sig2=sig2, alpha=alpha, theta = theta, plot=TRUE, col=rep("black",2), ylim=yl, xaxt="n", labels=F)
axis(1, at=seq(0, 80, 20), labels=seq(0, 8, 2))
title(xlab="Time (Ma)", ylab="Trait Value")
```

We can see from this visualization that the alpha parameter constrains the random walk of our trait around the value 0, which we defined as the optimum value (theta). 

*How does the OU model differ from the BM model in terms of the relationship between variance and time?* Hint: try plotting the following:

```{r}
par(mfrow=c(2,1), mar=c(4,4,1,1))
plot(x=1:80, apply(rw_bm, 2, var), xlab = "Time", ylab="Variance")
plot(x=1:80, y=apply(rw_ou, 2, var),  xlab = "Time", ylab="Variance")
```


Now let's finish off by repeating the OU simulations but with different model parameters 

```{r}
par(mfrow=c(2,2), mar=c(4,4,2,2))

# A. Original Parameters
time = 8
N = 50
sig2 = 0.09
alpha = 0.4
theta = 0
yl = c(-2, 2)
		
# simulate and plot
rw_ou = random_walks(model="OU_null", TIME=time, steps=10, nsim=N, sig2=sig2, alpha=alpha, theta = theta, plot=TRUE, col=rep("black",2), ylim=yl, xaxt="n", labels=F)
axis(1, at=seq(0, 80, 20), labels=seq(0, 8, 2))
title(xlab="Time (Ma)", ylab="Trait Value")

# B. Higher Alpha
time = 8
N = 100
sig2 = 0.09
alpha = 1.4
theta = 0
yl = c(-2, 2)
		
# simulate and plot
rw_ou = random_walks(model="OU_null", TIME=time, steps=10, nsim=N, sig2=sig2, 
alpha=alpha, theta = theta, plot=TRUE, col=rep("black",2), ylim=yl, xaxt="n", labels=F)
axis(1, at=seq(0, 80, 20), labels=seq(0, 8, 2))
title(xlab="Time (Ma)", ylab="Trait Value")

# C. Alpha = 0
time = 8
N = 100
sig2 = 0.09
alpha = 0
theta = 0
yl = c(-2, 2)
		
# simulate and plot
rw_ou = random_walks(model="OU_null", TIME=time, steps=10, nsim=N, sig2=sig2, alpha=alpha, theta = theta, plot=TRUE, col=rep("black",2), ylim=yl, xaxt="n", labels=F)
axis(1, at=seq(0, 80, 20), labels=seq(0, 8, 2))
title(xlab="Time (Ma)", ylab="Trait Value")

# D. Theta = 1.5 and alpha = 0.8
time = 8
N = 100
sig2 = 0.09
alpha = 0.8
theta = 1.5
yl = c(-2, 3)
		
# Simulate and plot
rw_ou = random_walks(model="OU_null", TIME=time, steps=10, nsim=N, sig2=sig2, alpha=alpha, theta = theta, plot=TRUE, col=rep("black",2), ylim=yl, xaxt="n", labels=F)
axis(1, at=seq(0, 80, 20), labels=seq(0, 8, 2))
title(xlab="Time (Ma)", ylab="Trait Value")

```

What you should be able to notice from the above visualizations is that when alpha = 0, the OU process becomes identical to a BM process. As alpha increases, the random walks are more tightly constrained about the optimum phenotype. 

```{r}
# Reset the plotting window
par(mfrow=c(1,1), mar=c(5, 4, 4, 2)+0.1)
```

